THE 4 MOST COMMON GREEDY PATTERNS ON LEETCODE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Again — these are the most frequently seen patterns, not an exhaustive list.
Greedy problems outside these patterns still exist. But if you master
these 4, you will handle the vast majority of what LeetCode throws at you.

PATTERN 1 — INTERVAL GREEDY → sort by end time, take what fits earliest
PATTERN 2 — SORT + ASSIGN → sort in a clever order, assign in that order
PATTERN 3 — RUNNING GREEDY → scan left to right, track a running value
PATTERN 4 — HEAP GREEDY → always pick the most urgent item available

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
PATTERN 1 — INTERVAL GREEDY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Select maximum number of non-overlapping activities
→ Minimum arrows/points to cover all intervals
→ Can you reach the end? / Minimum jumps to reach end?

The key insight: always pick the activity that ENDS the earliest.
Why? It leaves the maximum room for future activities.
Picking an activity that ends later blocks more future choices — never better.

EXAMPLE — Non-overlapping Intervals
https://leetcode.com/problems/non-overlapping-intervals

Find minimum number of intervals to REMOVE so none overlap.
Equivalently: find maximum number of non-overlapping intervals to KEEP.
Sort by END time. Greedily keep each interval if it doesn't overlap
with the last kept one (start >= lastEnd).
Removed = total - kept.
int eraseOverlapIntervals(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {
    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(),
         [](auto& a, auto& b){ return a[1] < b[1]; });  // sort by end

    int kept = 1, lastEnd = intervals[0][1];
    for (int i = 1; i < intervals.size(); i++) {
        if (intervals[i][0] >= lastEnd) {  // no overlap → keep it
            kept++;
            lastEnd = intervals[i][1];
        }
    }
    return intervals.size() - kept;
}
WHY END TIME? If two intervals both fit, the one ending earlier is always
at least as good. It takes up less "future space."

EXAMPLE — Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons
https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-arrows-to-burst-balloons

Same logic as non-overlapping intervals.
Sort by end. Shoot an arrow at the end of the first balloon.
Any balloon whose start <= this end is also burst (same arrow).
When a balloon starts after the current arrow → fire a new arrow.
int findMinArrowShots(vector<vector<int>>& points) {
    sort(points.begin(), points.end(),
         [](auto& a, auto& b){ return a[1] < b[1]; });

    int arrows = 1, arrowPos = points[0][1];
    for (int i = 1; i < points.size(); i++) {
        if (points[i][0] > arrowPos) {
            arrows++;
            arrowPos = points[i][1];
        }
    }
    return arrows;
}
EXAMPLE — Jump Game
https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game

Can you reach the last index?
Track the farthest index reachable so far.
If you're at an index beyond farthest → stuck, return false.
bool canJump(vector<int>& nums) {
    int farthest = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
        if (i > farthest) return false;
        farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
    }
    return true;
}
EXAMPLE — Jump Game II (minimum jumps)
https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game-ii

Track the farthest you can reach within the CURRENT jump.
When you exhaust the current jump range → you must jump.
At each forced jump, extend range to the farthest reachable so far.
int jump(vector<int>& nums) {
    int jumps = 0, curEnd = 0, farthest = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size() - 1; i++) {
        farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
        if (i == curEnd) {
            jumps++;
            curEnd = farthest;
        }
    }
    return jumps;
}
