COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Using greedy when DP is needed
Coin Change with greedy: picks largest coin first → wrong for many inputs.
Always ask: can a future step invalidate this choice? If yes → use DP.

MISTAKE 2 — Sorting by the wrong key in interval problems
Sort by START → useful for merging intervals.
Sort by END → correct for activity selection and arrow problems.
Mixing these gives wrong greedy decisions.

MISTAKE 3 — Forgetting the two-pass approach in Candy
One left-to-right pass handles left-neighbor constraints.
One right-to-left pass handles right-neighbor constraints.
A single pass cannot satisfy both directions at once.

MISTAKE 4 — Thinking greedy always needs sorting
Gas Station and Jump Game require no sorting.
The greedy insight is in the running variable and reset logic.

MISTAKE 5 — Not verifying feasibility in heap greedy problems
Reorganize String: if max frequency > ceil(n/2) → impossible.
Always check if a valid arrangement can exist before building it.

